BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2552@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T133728Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T100000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T103000
SUMMARY:mmu_notifier: can we use it better?
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:KVM MC · Claudio Imbrenda · https://lpc.events/event/20/contr
 ibutions/2552/\n\nKVM uses the mmu_notifier infrastructure to keep the gue
 st mapping up to date. After having completely rewritten the memory manage
 ment of KVM/s390 to use mmu_notifiers\, I have noticed some of the shortco
 mings in how KVM uses the notifiers.\n\nSome of areas where KVM's usage of
  the notifiers\, or the mmu_notifier infrastructure itself can be improved
 :\n\n - passing the reason code to the KVM callback when unmapping\n - mor
 e fine-grained reason codes\n - passing back flags from the notifier callb
 ack\n\nI will show how and why each of the above points would be useful fo
 r KVM with some concrete examples.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2552/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2555@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T133728Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T103000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T110000
SUMMARY:Exploring KVM and SMMUv3 Stage-2 Page Table Sharing for arm64 pKVM
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:KVM MC · Mostafa Saleh · https://lpc.events/event/20/contribu
 tions/2555/\n\nAs Protected KVM (pKVM) progresses on arm64\, achieving ful
 l isolation requires hypervisor-controlled IOMMUs to prevent DMA attacks. 
 While there has been progress on SMMUv3 support for pKVM via trap-and-emul
 ate on the mailing list [1]\, a major architectural design question remain
 s unresolved: managing the stage-2 translation tables.\n\nCurrently\, the 
 series relies on maintaining a shadow stage-2 page tables for the SMMUv3.\
 nArchitecturally\, it is possible to share these page tables between the C
 PU and the SMMUv3 in a similar way the kernel does SVA for stage-1\, which
  would reduce memory overhead. However\, KVM has different design constrai
 nts than user-space processes\, and we are expected to run on a wide range
  of hardware without introducing impractical limitations or assumptions br
 ought by SVA.\n\n**Some of the main points:**\n- Dealing with page faults 
 (Pinning\, BBM…)\n- IO TLB invalidation and DVM\n- Cache coherency of th
 e IOMMU and page tables\n\nThe goal of this session is to discuss the viab
 ility of shared stage-2 page tables and reach a path forward for DMA isola
 tion on pKVM.\n\n[1] https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260501111928.259252-1-s
 mostafa@google.com/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2555/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2551@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T133728Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T110000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T113000
SUMMARY:How to support multiple backends for guest_memfd?
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:KVM MC · Ackerley Tng · https://lpc.events/event/20/contribut
 ions/2551/\n\nTo improve performance of CoCo VMs\, there's active work on 
 huge pages for guest_memfd. The first "backend" in the works for providing
  huge pages is HugeTLB.\n\nFrank summarized interest in having devdax/ZONE
 _DEVICE memory as another backend [1] for guest_memfd.\n\nIf guest_memfd i
 s to be *the* guest memory provider of KVM\, it has to support (almost) an
 y memory backend that can be configured in a memslot today through VMAs\, 
 going beyond memory backends to other things can be mmap()-ed\, like perha
 ps MMIO though VFIO.\n\nI would like to get ideas and feedback on how gues
 t_memfd should be extended to support different backends.\n\nAre flags wha
 t we really want to go with? Will we end up supporting many flags?  Using 
 HugeTLB as an example\, flags/fields can encode use of HugeTLB\, HugeTLB p
 age sizes\, but how would the HugeTLB mount be encoded? Should there be so
 me way to encode size limits of the mount? Is that a required feature?\n\n
 Could we perhaps reuse existing interfaces to communicate the memory confi
 g to guest_memfd? What if we hand fds (opened on specific mounts) where gu
 est_memfd can grab config from? Should guest_memfd perhaps use pre-allocat
 ed memory from the fd?\n\n[1] https://lore.kernel.org/all/CAPTztWajm_JLpp9
 BjRcX=h72r25ELrXeGkOXVachybBxLJGS=g@mail.gmail.com/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2551/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2553@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T133728Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T120000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T123000
SUMMARY:In-Kernel Caretaker for Orphaned VMs
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:KVM MC · Pasha Tatashin · https://lpc.events/event/20/contrib
 utions/2553/\n\nAs cloud infrastructure continues to push toward zero-down
 time host maintenance\, extending the capabilities of kexec-based Live Upd
 ate to minimize guest disruption is becoming increasingly critical. This p
 roposal introduces the architectural concept of an "Orphaned VM"—a virtu
 al machine that actively executes guest instructions on isolated physical 
 hardware while completely decoupled from a host operating system or a VMM 
 during a host reboot. This execution asymmetry is achieved by introducing 
 the "Caretaker\," a specialized bare-metal primitive interpose layer that 
 runs within the hypervisor's privileged hardware state to trap and resolve
  some VM exits locally while the host kernel is offline. This session will
  explore the mechanisms required to orchestrate this transition using the 
 LUO\, focusing on how vcpufd structures are preserved across the reboot ga
 p\, how physical CPUs are shielded from boot-time reset signals\, and how 
 asynchronous hardware challenges like timekeeping drift\, stray interrupts
 \, and guest-to-guest IPI routing are mitigated during the management gap.
 \n\nRFC Reference & Discussion Thread: For full architectural background a
 nd ongoing community feedback\, see the original mailing list discussion a
 t https://lore.kernel.org/all/afEwWZksU0Fw61oT@plex
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2553/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2554@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T133728Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T123000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T130000
SUMMARY:Orphaned VMs: Which VM Exits can we handle?
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:KVM MC · Pratyush Yadav\, Tarun Sahu · https://lpc.events/eve
 nt/20/contributions/2554/\n\nOrphaned VMs [0] is proposed to be the next e
 volution of hypervisor live update. It relies on a specialized micro-hyper
 visor\, called the Caretaker\, which handles VM Exits during the time betw
 een old kernel shutting down and new kernel booting up. The Caretaker redu
 ces the downtime observed by the VM during live update by servicing VM Exi
 ts during this transition period.\n\nThe Caretaker is not a full hyperviso
 r or VMM\, so it cannot service all the VM Exits. If it cannot handle a VM
  Exit\, it must pause the VM and wait for the next kernel to take over. Th
 e success of the Caretaker in reducing observed VM downtime relies on whic
 h VM Exits it can and can't service\, and how frequently they occur.\n\nIn
  this talk\, we will present the types and frequency of VM Exits observed 
 under some sample workloads. We will also categorize the VM Exits into one
 s which the Caretaker can handle and the ones which it can't.\n\nBy combin
 ing the frequency of VM Exits and whether they can be handled\, we can est
 imate observed VM downtime\, and decide how effective Orhpaned VMs can be.
 \n\n[0] https://lore.kernel.org/all/afEwWZksU0Fw61oT@plex/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2554/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2632@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T133728Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T130000
